course: dictionary
lesson: 9688
title: Sufficiently means enough
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sufficiently is a formal word, and it means enough: as much as is needed. «The water was sufficiently warm to swim» tells you the water was warm enough to swim in.
explain: Look for the clue that comes next. A word like sufficiently often answers the question enough for what? In «make it sufficiently hot», the heat must be high enough for the job to work.
explain: Try the swap test. Put enough in the same place: «make it hot enough» means the same as «make it sufficiently hot». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Sufficiently means enough, as much as is needed.
fact: Sufficiently is an adverb, so it tells you how far something goes, as in «sufficiently large».
fact: The adjective is sufficient, and the noun is sufficiency.
fact: «Sufficiently» is often used before an adjective, such as «sufficiently clear» or «sufficiently hot».
fact: «Not sufficiently» means not enough.
exercise: «The water was sufficiently warm to swim.» What does sufficiently mean here? | Only a little bit warm, so no one swam | Boiling hot | Freezing cold | *Warm enough to swim
exercise: Mia says sufficiently is just a fancy way of saying very. Is she right? | No, it means nothing at all | Yes, it means extremely fast | Yes, it means very | *No, it means enough, not very
exercise: In the phrase «sufficiently large», which word is the adjective? | sufficiently | *large | both | neither
test: «The pan was not sufficiently hot, so the butter did not melt.» What does not sufficiently hot mean? | Hot and sizzling | Cold and wet | Too hot to touch | *Not hot enough
test: Sufficiently is the formal word for which plain word? | very | *enough | soon | never
exam: In «the tent was sufficiently large for four children», what does sufficiently mean? | Far away from the children | *Big enough for four children | Bright and colourful | Very small
